# How many places a program can write divert the machinery into running
# the program's own procedure.
#
# Every other register here asks whether an object is sealed. This one
# asks what the sealing is for, and asks it the way a program would:
# tests/machinery_diverted_test.ps plants a procedure of its own
# everywhere a program can reach and write, drives the machinery, and
# counts the places that ran it.
#
# The two questions are not the same, and the difference is why this
# exists. A device class is sealed and its instance is sealed, and a
# program can still hand the machinery a whole device of its own by
# writing the slot that names one: every object on that route is closed
# and a census of objects reads clean.
#
# NO PAIR DIVERTS. The one that did is closed, and what closed it is
# written here so that a rise says what came back:
#
#   a device of the program's own / showpage
#       The machinery found the device through a slot the graphics state
#       holds, and the graphics state was reached from privatedict, which
#       is reachable by decision -- driver prologs name it. The slot has
#       to stay writable: a glyph device and a capture device are swapped
#       in through it. So the dictionary holding the slot is rooted in
#       the context instead, where the machinery and the collector reach
#       it and nothing a program enumerates names it.
#
# One pair was reported here and was never real. The recorder class was
# counted while the case before it had left a device of its own in place
# carrying the same marker, so what ran was that device's method. The
# cases put back what they replace now, and the recorder is asked first.
#
# The recorder is published closed like every other class. It was held
# open on the reading that each build writes its method suite into the
# class; the write goes into a dictionary the build makes for the target
# it is specialising for, and the published class is finished before it
# is published.
#
#   count

diverted 0
